::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve the system y \= x + 3 and y \= 2x + 1.
{
=A. (2, 5)#Correct. Set x + 3 \= 2x + 1 to get x \= 2, then y \= 5.
~B. (1, 4)#This point satisfies y \= x + 3 but not y \= 2x + 1.
~C. (3, 6)#This point satisfies y \= x + 3 but not the second equation.
~D. (2, 4)#The x-value is correct, but y should be 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Which point solves 2x + y \= 7 and x - y \= 2?
{
~A. (1, 3)#This reverses the coordinates and fails the second equation.
=B. (3, 1)#Correct. Substituting gives 2(3)+1\=7 and 3-1\=2.
~C. (2, 3)#This satisfies neither equation completely.
~D. (4, -1)#This satisfies x - y \= 5, not 2.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y \= x² and y \= 4.
{
~A. (4, 16)#This point is on y \= x² but not y \= 4.
~B. (0, 4)#This is on y \= 4 but not y \= x².
=C. (-2, 4) and (2, 4)#Correct. Set x² \= 4, so x \= -2 or x \= 2.
~D. (2, 2)#This point is not on either equation as stated.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
What does an intersection point of two graphs represent in a system?
{
~A. A point that satisfies only the first equation#A system solution must satisfy every equation.
~B. The y-intercept of both graphs#Intersections are not always y-intercepts.
~C. A point where neither equation is true#That cannot be a system solution.
=D. A point that satisfies both equations#Correct. At an intersection, both equations have the same x- and y-values.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y \= x + 1 and y \= x² - 1.
{
=A. (-1, 0) and (2, 3)#Correct. Set x + 1 \= x² - 1, so x² - x - 2 \= 0.
~B. (0, 1) and (1, 0)#These points do not satisfy both equations.
~C. (1, 2) only#This point satisfies y \= x + 1 but not the quadratic.
~D. No solution#The line and parabola intersect twice.
}

